### What is the Client Credentials flow?

The Client Credentials flow is the simplest OAuth2 grant. It is a single back-channel call: the client sends its own credentials to the token endpoint and receives an access token. There is no user, no redirect, no consent screen, and no authorization code.

This flow is used for machine-to-machine communication: cron jobs, batch processes, backend services calling other backend services.

```text
POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Authorization: Basic base64(client_id:client_secret)

grant_type=client_credentials&scope=fraud:check

Response:
{
  "access_token": "eyJhbGciOi...",
  "token_type": "bearer",
  "expires_in": 3600,
  "scope": "fraud:check"
}
```

### How do you implement it in Spring?

Spring Security's OAuth2 client support handles Client Credentials with `WebClient` or `RestClient` that automatically fetches and caches the token.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.web.reactive.function.client.ServletOAuth2AuthorizedClientExchangeFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class FraudCheckClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public WebClient fraudCheckWebClient(
            OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager clientManager) {
        var filter = new ServletOAuth2AuthorizedClientExchangeFilterFunction(
            clientManager);
        filter.setDefaultClientRegistrationId("fraud-service");
        return WebClient.builder()
            .baseUrl("https://fraud.nationalbank.in")
            .apply(filter.oauth2Configuration())
            .build();
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          fraud-service:
            client-id: settlement-service
            client-secret: ${FRAUD_CLIENT_SECRET}
            authorization-grant-type: client_credentials
            scope: fraud:check
        provider:
          fraud-service:
            token-uri: https://auth.nationalbank.in/oauth2/token
```

### How does the Resource Server enforce scopes?

The Resource Server validates the token and checks that it carries the required scope. The settlement service can only call endpoints protected by the `fraud:check` scope.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import org.springframework.security.access.prepost.PreAuthorize;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class FraudCheckController {

    @PostMapping("/api/v1/fraud/check")
    @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('SCOPE_fraud:check')")
    public FraudResult checkTransaction(@RequestBody TransactionRequest request) {
        System.out.println("Checking fraud for amount: " + request.getAmount());
        return new FraudResult(false, "Transaction appears legitimate.");
    }

    @PostMapping("/api/v1/accounts/close")
    @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('SCOPE_account:close')")
    public String closeAccount(@RequestBody String accountId) {
        return "Account " + accountId + " closed.";
    }
}
```

```text
Settlement service (scope=fraud:check):
  POST /api/v1/fraud/check   --> 200 OK (scope matches)
  POST /api/v1/accounts/close --> 403 Forbidden (scope missing)
```

### How is Client Credentials different from Authorization Code?

| Aspect | Client Credentials | Authorization Code |
|---|---|---|
| User involved | No | Yes |
| Token represents | The service itself | The user (delegated) |
| Consent screen | No | Yes |
| Redirect needed | No | Yes |
| Refresh token | No | Yes |
| Use case | Backend-to-backend, cron jobs | Web app login, user-facing |
| Channel | Back-channel only | Front + back channel |

```java
// Client Credentials: service authenticates as itself
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.http.HttpEntity;
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.util.LinkedMultiValueMap;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;

public class ClientCredentialsTokenFetcher {

    public String fetchToken() {
        RestTemplate rest = new RestTemplate();
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.setBasicAuth("settlement-service", "service_secret_123");

        var body = new LinkedMultiValueMap<String, String>();
        body.add("grant_type", "client_credentials");
        body.add("scope", "fraud:check");

        var response = rest.postForObject(
            "https://auth.nationalbank.in/oauth2/token",
            new HttpEntity<>(body, headers), java.util.Map.class);

        String token = (String) response.get("access_token");
        System.out.println("Token obtained for settlement-service.");
        return token;
    }
}
```

### Why is there no refresh token?

Refresh tokens exist to keep a user session alive without re-login. In Client Credentials there is no user session. When the token expires, the service simply requests a new one with its own credentials. This is cheap and instant because there is no user interaction.

```java
// When the token expires, just fetch a new one:
public String callFraudApi() {
    String token = tokenCache.getOrFetch(); // fetches new if expired
    // Use token in Authorization: Bearer header
    return webClient.post()
        .uri("/api/v1/fraud/check")
        .headers(h -> h.setBearerAuth(token))
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(String.class)
        .block();
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is hardcoding the client secret in source code. Secrets must come from a secrets manager like Vault or cloud-native secrets (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault) and be rotated regularly.

```java
// WRONG: secret hardcoded
String clientSecret = "my_secret_123"; // in source code!

// CORRECT: secret from environment or secrets manager
String clientSecret = System.getenv("FRAUD_CLIENT_SECRET");
// Or injected via Spring's ${FRAUD_CLIENT_SECRET} property
```

The second mistake is granting overly broad scopes. The settlement service should only get `fraud:check`, not `account:write` or `*`.

### What about higher-assurance alternatives?

For higher security, banks prefer alternatives to a shared client_secret:

- **Mutual TLS (mTLS)**: both the client and server present certificates, so the client authenticates via its certificate rather than a shared secret.
- **Signed JWT client assertion**: the client signs a JWT with its own private key and sends it as the `client_assertion` parameter, proving possession of the key without sharing a secret.

```text
// Signed JWT client assertion (private_key_jwt):
POST /oauth2/token
  grant_type=client_credentials
  &scope=fraud:check
  &client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer
  &client_assertion=eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiJ9...
  // JWT is signed with the client's private key
  // Server verifies with the client's registered public key
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers know that Client Credentials tokens should be cached until near-expiry, not fetched on every request, to avoid hammering the Authorization Server. They also know that even internal services must follow least-privilege scoping, and that the flow is only for confidential clients: a browser or mobile app must never use Client Credentials because the secret would be exposed.
